\par\par \b \uc2 Indonesia c.bank signs $10 bln financial stability deal with Singapore\b0\par\par\uc2 182 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 November 2018\par 05:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2018 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Nov 5 (Reuters) - The central banks of Indonesia and Singapore on Monday said they had agreed a bilateral deal for a $10 billion backstop to help maintain monetary and financial stability after a recent bout of turbulence in markets.\par \par \uc2 The pact, which will be in place for one year, comprises a local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap agreement of around $7 billion equivalent and another $3 billion that allows for repurchase transactions between the two central banks to obtain U.S. dollar cash using government bonds of major countries as collateral.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been recently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stabilise its rupiah \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  which fell to 20-year lows against the U.S. dollar amid a global rout in emerging markets.\par \par \uc2 "Economic fundamentals in the regional economies remain sound. But markets can sometimes overreact in the face of heightened uncertainty. This bilateral financial arrangement will instil confidence amongst investors," Singapore's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief \uc2 Ravi Menon\uc2  said. (Reporting by John Geddie Editing by Shri Navaratnam) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2018-11-5T05:38:25.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020181105eeb500gl9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
