\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-"Innocent bystanders": Emerging economies struggle to contain capital outflows\b0\par\par\uc2 803 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Oktober 2018\par 01:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2018 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Repeats item issued late Sunday, with no change to text)               \par \par \uc2 By Ed Davies, Sumeet Chatterjee and Gayatri Suroyo               \par \par \uc2 NUSA DUA, Indonesia, Oct 14 (Reuters) - After suffering months of capital outflows, policy makers from emerging markets attending IMF-World Bank meetings in Indonesia had a message for leading economies: current monetary and trade policies risk undermining us all.               \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 International Monetary Fund-World Bank\uc2  meetings wrapping up on Sunday gave central bankers and finance ministers from around the world a chance to meet face-to-face in Indonesia, whose rupiah \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit a new 20-year low this week.               \par \par \uc2 Poorer and populous emerging markets have been particularly vulnerable to the escalating U.S.-Sino tariff war and rate rises by the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Investors dumped assets seen as riskier, sparking painful \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  plunges that have punished countries from India to recession-hit South Africa, as well as triggering crises in Turkey and Argentina.               \par \par \uc2 "We are all aware that the normalisation of the monetary policy in the U.S., combined with their fiscal policy and trade policy ... are all creating a systemic impact to the whole economy in the world," Indonesian Finance Minister \uc2 Sri Mulyani Indrawati\uc2  said in an interview during the meetings in Bali.               \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's nearly three-year-old tightening cycle has in part prompted a global shift in capital away from emerging markets and after three hikes this year, it foresees another December rise, three more next year, and one in 2020.               \par \par \uc2 A senior Fed official in Bali said the rate rises were right for domestic policy and ensuring they were gradual and predictable was "the best solution" for minimising unintended volatility in emerging markets.               \par \par \uc2 In a bid to support the rupiah, \uc2 Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  has raised rates five times since mid-May and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  regularly, but still the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has lost nearly 11 percent this year, leaving it at the weakest levels since the 1998 Asian financial crisis.               \par \par \uc2 Bank Indonesia Governor \uc2 Perry Warjiyo\uc2  said that the 150 basis point rate hikes since mid-May aimed to keep Indonesian assets attractive enough for foreigners to stay invested, but calibrating this in the current environment was hard.               \par \par \uc2 "The risk premia are very difficult to incorporate because risk premia are responding to geopolitical, responding to trade tension," Warjiyo told a panel in Bali.                              \par \par \uc2 "SPILLOVER" RISKS               \par \par \uc2 Finance ministers for developing nations in the Group of 24 economies urged major economies to reform the global trading system, rather than discard it.               \par \par \uc2 The G24 statement, issued on the sidelines of the Bali meetings, said all emerging markets were "adversely affected" by excessive capital flow volatility.               \par \par \uc2 While many countries shared common fears, Indrawati said it was difficult to forge cooperation to counter the risks.               \par \par \uc2 "It's not really clear how the world is going to coordinate more effectively, especially when each country has their own domestic issues," she said.               \par \par \uc2 The Philippine peso has shed nearly 8 percent this year and its deputy \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor, Diwa Guinigundo, said the \uc2 IMF\uc2  and other global institutions should advise advanced economies on the potential negative consequences of their moves.               \par \par \uc2 "It's the spillovers that we are concerned with ... the spillover could have effects from one market to the other, from the financial market to the real market," said Guinigundo.               \par \par \uc2 He said while it was good for policymakers to be ahead of the curve in tightening, they should take account of the growing clout of emerging markets. "It should also be emphasized that ASEAN+3 (China, Japan, Korea), we account for a good bulk of the world's population and GDP."                              \par \par \uc2 "EITHER ALL ARE WINNERS OR ALL ARE LOSERS"               \par \par \uc2 IMF Managing Director \uc2 Christine Lagarde\uc2  urged members at the meeting to "de-escalate" trade tensions and work on fixing global trade rules. She also warned against adding \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the trade conflict, saying this would hurt global growth as well as "innocent bystander" nations, including emerging markets that supply commodities to China.               \par \par \uc2 Egyptian Finance Minister Mohamed Maait said policy makers in developed countries should understand that if their actions hurt other countries it would have knock-on effects.               \par \par \uc2 "You need me. I am a market for you, I am an opportunity for you," Maait told Reuters. "I don't believe there will be a winner and a loser. Either all are winners or all are losers."               \par \par \uc2 Indeed, market ructions have now cascaded through to developed markets with Wall Street seeing a six session slide until a rebound on Friday, amid fears over the trade war between China and the United States.               \par \par \uc2 However, other than having effective monetary policies, developing markets can do little to cope with the impact of rate hikes and trade battles, said \uc2 Jacob Frenkel\uc2 , chairman of JPMorgan Chase International.               \par \par \uc2 Released: 2018-10-15T00:30:00.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020181014eeae015fp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw77\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1155\pichgoal600
