\par\par \b \uc2 POLL-Strong US economy buoys dollar, puts Asian  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  on skids\b0\par\par\uc2 921 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Oktober 2018\par 10:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2018 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     * Short positions on most \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rise on stronger dollar\par     * Bearish bets on rupee, rupiah at over 5-year high\par     * India rate decision, Philippine inflation data awaited\par \par     By Rushil Dutta\par     Oct 4 (Reuters) - Investors continued to bet that Asian\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would weaken against the dollar, a Reuters poll\par showed, as hawkish comments by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  policy makers and\par a steady rate hike path buttressed the greenback.\par     Fed Chairman \f28 \uc2 \f2 Jerome Powell\f28 \uc2 \f2  said on Wednesday the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central\par bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may raise interest rates above an estimated "neutral"\par setting as the "remarkably positive" economy continues to grow.\par \par     This came after a survey showed the U.S. services sector\par activity raced to a 21-year high last month, boosting\par expectations that Friday's payroll report could surprise.\par \par     The dollar index against a basket of six major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par  has gained nearly 1 percent since the Fed last Wednesday\par raised rates as expected and provided an upbeat view of the\par world's biggest economy. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said it foresees\par another rate hike in December, three more next year and one in\par 2020.  \par     The rosy economic view and a steady policy tightening path\par have largely helped drive up Treasury yields, which rose to over\par 7-year highs on Thursday, adding further pressure on Asian\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     Rising yields have been troublesome for emerging assets as\par they tend to draw away foreign funds. Emerging Asian countries\par with ballooning current account deficits due to expensive oil\par imports, like India and Indonesia, have been the biggest\par casualties so far this year.\par     The poll of 11 respondents showed investors increased their\par bearish bets on the Indonesian rupiah over the past two weeks to\par a more than five-year high, while raising short positions in the\par Indian rupee slightly.\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has tried to shore up the rupiah via\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and rate hikes. Top government officials also\par pitched in with positive comments. The finance minister said on\par Tuesday that the economy was adjusting "quite well" to higher\par U.S. interest rates and the fall of the rupiah, asserting that\par many of its economic indicators were good. \par     The rupiah was down 0.7 percent as of 0557 GMT and\par has weakened over 10 percent so far this year, making it the\par second biggest loser in the region after the Indian rupee\par , which has continued to plumb new lows and forced the \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     "Despite a stronger dollar, crude oil prices continued to\par remain very elevated, which is putting further pressure on\par current account deficit \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  that rely on oil imports,\par India and Indonesia specifically," said Khoon Goh, head of Asia\par research at ANZ. \par     "The INR hit yet another record low today and in the near\par term, it doesn't look like there's any respite coming." \par     At its policy meeting on Friday, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of India\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par is expected to raise rates for a third time since June to check\par inflationary pressures as it grapples with a depreciating\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and surging oil prices.\par     Short positions on the Philippine peso also piled up\par as the country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  struggles to tame stubbornly high\par inflation that undermines its GDP growth target.\par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will be looking out for September inflation\par data due on Friday, after it raised benchmark rates last week\par and said it was open to further tightening.\par     Annual inflation was expected to have risen last month,\par moving close to 7 percent, a Reuters poll showed, against the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's target of 2 percent to 4 percent. \par     The Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  positioning poll is focused on what\par analysts and fund managers believe are the current market\par positions in nine Asian emerging market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : the Chinese\par yuan, South Korean won, Singapore dollar, Indonesian rupiah,\par Taiwan dollar, Indian rupee, Philippine peso, Malaysian ringgit\par and the Thai baht.\par     The poll uses estimates of net long or short positions on a\par scale of minus 3 to plus 3. A score of plus 3 indicates the\par market is significantly long U.S. dollars.\par     The figures include positions held through non-deliverable\par forwards (NDFs).\par     The survey findings are provided below (positions\par in U.S. dollar versus each \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ):     \par \par  DDMM  CNY    KRW    SGD   IDR   TWD   INR   MYR   PHP   THB\par  4/10  1.11   0.56   0.70  1.65  0.65  1.74  0.74  1.53  0.10\par  20/9  1.12   0.79   0.95  1.57  0.69  1.72  0.82  1.44  0.33\par  6/9   0.92   0.64   0.86  1.37  0.68  1.72  0.90  1.13  0.24\par  23/8  0.93   0.71   0.84  1.27  0.91  1.19  0.88  0.86  0.47\par  9/8   1.52   0.94   0.80  1.21  0.99  1     0.86  1.02  0.66\par  26/7  1.55   1.08   0.84  1.35  1.13  1.25  0.76  1.44  0.87\par  12/7  1.00   0.91   0.68  1.31  0.88  1.18  0.64  1.35  0.70\par  28/6  1.28   1.03   0.94  1.32  1.03  1.43  0.94  1.58  0.73\par  31/5  -0.15  -0.17  0.39  1.06  0.33  1.42  0.69  1.25  0.06\par  17/5  -0.28  -0.33  0.28  1.08  0.07  1.31  0.21  0.68  -0.14\par  \par     \par  (Adittional reporting by Ambar Warrick and Nikhil Nainan in\par Bengaluru; Editing by Subhranshu Sahu)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2018-10-4T09:24:32.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020181004eea40119t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
