\par\par \b \uc2 Indonesian import-cutting measures could dent growth - \uc2 World Bank\b0\par\par\uc2 462 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 September 2018\par 12:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2018 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA, Sept 20 (Reuters) - Efforts by Indonesian authorities to significantly narrow the current account deficit to help the rupiah might not be effective and could crimp economic growth, the \uc2 World Bank\uc2  said on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Indonesia's government and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  have taken measures to reduce the pressure on the rupiah as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  recently plunged to its weakest level in 20 years, caught up in an emerging market sell-off.\par \par \uc2 Steps such as raising import taxes, delaying billions of dollars of infrastructure projects and widening the use of biodiesel are intended to cut imports and reduce a yawning current account deficit, which in the second quarter widened to 3 percent of GDP, the most in nearly four years.\par \par \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  has raised interest rates by 125 basis points since mid-May and spent billions in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the rupiah.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 World Bank\uc2 , in its "\b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  Economic Quarterly" report, said the import taxes on more than 1,000 items, mostly consumer goods, and delays in state investments are "unlikely to have a large impact on the current account in the near term".\par \par \uc2 "Given the significant infrastructure gap in the country, these measures may have the unintended effects of weighing on potential growth and rendering longer-term consequences for the economy," the multilateral lender said, adding that tighter fiscal and monetary policy both will weigh on growth in the immediate and medium-term.\par \par \uc2 Higher tariffs on consumer goods could also increase inflation and hurt private consumption, the bank said.\par \par \uc2 TINY 2019 FORECAST TRIM\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 World Bank\uc2  maintained its forecast for Indonesia's economy to grow 5.2 percent this year, and revised its 2019 forecast marginally to 5.2 percent from 5.3 percent.\par \par \uc2 Last year, the Washington-based bank estimated that Indonesia would need to invest $500 billion over five years to fill its infrastructure gap.\par \par \uc2 In response to the \uc2 World Bank\uc2 's new report, Indonesia's planning minister \uc2 Bambang Brodjonegoro\uc2  said the government is trying to solve a current account problem for the long term, not only for the near term.\par \par \uc2 At the report's launch, World Bank Indonesia country director Rodrigo Chavez described the rupiah's 9 percent drop against the U.S. dollar this year as a "moderate depreciation".\par \par \uc2 He said that the rate should be seen as a "source of confidence" as the depreciation was less in nominal terms than other emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  had, leading to a real effective appreciation in the second quarter - although the report noted that the rupiah has fallen again in the third quarter.\par \par \uc2 Risks associated with a financial crisis for Indonesia remain small, it added. (Reporting by Tabita Diela; Writing by Gayatri Suroyo; Editing by Richard Borsuk) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2018-9-20T11:16:41.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020180920ee9k01lhi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
