\par\par \b \uc2 Indonesian Rupiah Falls Through 2015 Low -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,244 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 September 2018\par 06:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0441 GMT - Fresh selling of the Indonesian rupiah has the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at its lowest level versus the dollar since the Asian financial crisis 20 years ago. The dollar is up 0.4% at IDR14,777, according to \uc2 Thomson Reuters\uc2  , putting the rupiah's 2018 slide at more than 8% against the greenback. This as \uc2 Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Friday it has been actively \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Other emerging-market units, like the Turkish lira and South African rand, are also down versus the dollar today. (saumya.vaishampayan@wsj.com; @saumvaish)\par \par \uc2 0432 GMT - Total loans in Hong Kong grew at the slowest pace in 18 months in July at 9.5%, putting the total at HK$9.77 trillion ($1.24 trillion). \uc2 OCBC\uc2  thinks increases may reach 5% by year-end due to China's economic slowdown, trade tensions and prospects for higher local interest rates. The investment bank adds that with monetary-policy divergence between the US and China, offshore financing may become less attractive to mainland companies. As such, "banks' need to scramble for funds may become less imminent." Still, \uc2 OCBC\uc2  says their net-interest margin could remain pressured given the strong growth of Hong Kong dollar-fixed deposits and the expected uptrend of the Hibor rate benchmark. \uc2 OCBC\uc2  expects the city's banks to raise their prime lending rate a quarter-point by year's end. It hasn't changed in a decade. (chester.yung@wsj.com; @chester_yung)\par \par \uc2 0308 GMT - While little change is generally expected in the wording of tomorrow's policy statement, \uc2 Australia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could still go from saying the next rate move is "likely" to be higher to the chance of going up or down is "evenly balanced," says Amp GFX strategist \uc2 Greg Gibbs\uc2 . That would being a market shock, and result in a noted hit to the Aussie dollar and interest rates. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0304 GMT - Taiwan manufacturing \uc2 PMI\uc2  hit a 22-month low of 53.0 in August, versus July's 53.1, with index complier \uc2 IHS Markit\uc2  saying the results point to a possible growth slowdown in the months ahead. Production and new orders both rose last month, helped by a solid increase in export sales, but "companies failed to increase their staffing levels for the first time in over 5 years" last month, the firm notes. \uc2 IHS Markit\uc2  says worries about China-US trade may weigh on the production outlook. (chester.yung@wsj.com; @chester_yung)\par \par \uc2 0224 GMT - Although Australia's home-price downturn has already lasted longer than the 12-month average of the 7 previous slides since 1980, Capital Economics notes the 3% drop is the smallest so far. But it contends that the full effect of credit-tightening and mortgage-rate hikes have yet to be felt, so the firm thinks this downturn could prove to be the longest and deepest in Australia's modern history. It predicts a cumulative 12% home-price drop over 4 years. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0211 GMT - Kuroda saying he won't raise rates for a long time are aimed at controlling expectations for increases, says \uc2 Merrill Lynch\uc2  rate strategist Shuichi Ohsaki. The BoJ's chief's comments were part of in an interview with Japan's Yomiuri newspaper published on Saturday. That as Ohsaki says the BoJ slowing down bond-buying this month could send too strong a signal for a possible exit from current ultraeasy policy. JGB yields have been broadly higher this morning after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  indicated a slower pace of buying of 3-to-10-year debt, and Ohsaki says the BoJ may well slow purchases for super-long bonds in the future. (kosaku.narioka@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0206 GMT - After 2 months of fairly strong growth, Australian retail sales were flat in July, with food-related firms seeing growth while declines were logged in clothing, household goods and department stores. The data confirm it is still a very-patchy environment for retail in general as households continue to battle against a number of headwinds. Last week's mortgage-rate rise by \uc2 Westpac\uc2  will add to the drags being created by stagnant real wages, slow growth in other sources of household income and falling home prices, says Oxford Economics. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0158 GMT - Ongoing home-price declines in Sydney and Melbourne will ultimately depress consumer spending as homeowners will be less inclined to allow their savings rate to decline, predicts AMP Capital economist \uc2 Shane Oliver\uc2  . That's consistent with the firm's view that \uc2 Australia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't increase interest rates for at least 2 more years. Oliver says home-price weakness is at levels where the \uc2 RBA\uc2  started cutting rates in 2008 and 2011, "so we still can't rule out the next move in rates being a cut rather than a hike." (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0151 GMT - Australia's job market may have softened last month with advertisements easing 0.6% from July to offset roughly half that month's bounce, says ANZ. That as year-over-year the ad increase slowed to 5.1%. Economist \uc2 David Plank\uc2  notes job ads have followed a saw-tooth pattern in seasonally adjusted terms, leaving the level little different from the start of the year. He adds while the level of job ads is still consistent with further employment growth, it's no longer pointing to a further decline in unemployment. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0136 GMT - Malaysian PM Mahathir's recent tirade against Chinese investors--especially if he continues the rhetoric--isn't expected to be good for the Southeast Asian country. Koon Yew Yin, a prominent Malaysian investor and one of the founders of constructions giants \uc2 IJM\uc2  and \uc2 Gamuda\uc2 , says Mahathir should realize he's sending the wrong signal to foreign investors and purchasers of properties in Malaysia by pushing an ultranationalist position on the $100 billion Forest City project. Koon says he's not alone as even the PM's office has clarified that foreigners are allowed to make Malaysia their place of permanent residence under the Malaysia My Second Home scheme, an international residency program started under Mahathir's regime in the early 2000s. (yantoultra.ngui@wsj.com; @yantoultra)\par \par \uc2 0128 GMT - JGB yields have risen as the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  's September bond-buying schedule indicates a slower pace of purchases for 3-to-10-year bonds. While increasing the upper end of potential offer sizes, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  says it will cut the number of scheduled \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The BoJ may also buy bonds on dates it hasn't scheduled to do so. Ten-year yields have risen to a 1-month high of 0.115%. Meanwhile, the yen is at session highs, putting the dollar at Y110.93 versus around Y111.20 earlier, and the Nikkei is down 0.5%. (kosaku.narioka@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0123 GMT - China guided the yuan weaker against the dollar on Monday following the greenback's end-of-week gains. The \uc2 PBOC\uc2  fixed the dollar's midpoint for daily trading at CNY6.8347, compared with CNY6.8246 on Friday. The dollar ended the week's onshore trading at CNY6.8299. (saumya.vaishampayan@wsj.com; @saumvaish)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 03, 2018 00:42 ET (04:42 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020180903ee9300085\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
