\par\par \b \uc2 Fitch affirms Indonesia's 'BBB' rating, maintains stable outlook\b0\par\par\uc2 272 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 September 2018\par 05:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2018 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Sept 3 (Reuters) - \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  affirmed its long-term foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  issuer default rating (IDR) on Indonesia at 'BBB' and maintained a stable outlook, citing low government debt burden, favourable growth outlook and structural indicators below rating peers.\par \par \uc2 The monetary and exchange-rate policy of \uc2 Indonesia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has helped in stabilising foreign capital flows, the rating agency said in a report released on Monday, adding that the economy's asset markets will remain vulnerable to "market jitters" due to possibilities of a widening current account deficit and dependence on foreign capital.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Southeast Asia's biggest economy stepped up \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and bond \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in an attempt to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  rupiah as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  neared levels not seen since the Asian financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 Ongoing global trade tensions could cause Indonesia's current account deficit to widen further than expected earlier, Fitch said.\par \par \uc2 The rating agency said it expected Indonesia's gross domestic product (GDP) growth to rise to 5.2 percent in 2019 and 5.3 percent in 2020, from 5.1 percent in 2018, supported by the continued public infrastructure spending push.\par \par \uc2 "However, uncertainty surrounding these forecasts has increased as investment growth has been decelerating in recent quarters and rising interest rates may dampen growth," Fitch said.\par \par \uc2 In December, Fitch raised Indonesia's rating to 'BBB' from 'BBB-', with a stable outlook, on the back of economic and monetary policies that made the economy resilient to external shocks. (Reporting by Kanishka Singh in Bengaluru; Editing by Gopakumar Warrier) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2018-9-3T04:41:36.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020180903ee9300af1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
