\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2- \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia \highlight\uc2  rupiah nears 1998 crisis levels, c.bank  \highlight22\uc2 intervenes \highlight\uc2  in  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 , bonds markets\b0\par\par\uc2 601 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 August 2018\par 11:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2018 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * Rupiah falls to 14,730/dollar, near 1998 level\par \par \uc2 * Yield of 10-yr bond at highest since Dec 2016\par \par \uc2 * Exporters unwilling to sell dollars - analyst (Adds c.bank gov comment, background)\par \par \uc2 By Gayatri Suroyo and Maikel Jefriando\par \par \uc2 JAKARTA, Aug 31 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped up \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and bond \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday to defend the rupiah, as contagion from an emerging market sell-off pushed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  close to levels not seen since the Asian financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 The rupiah slipped 0.3 percent to 14,730 per dollar on Friday, matching its weakest since September 2015. If it weakens further, it would be at levels last seen in 1998. Nonetheless, some analysts say Southeast Asia's biggest economy is in better shape to withstand what has been a gradual \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciation compared with the 1997-1998 crisis, when the rupiah plummeted from around 2,000 to an all-time low of 16,800.\par \par \uc2 "\uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2 's (BI) commitment to maintain economic stability, especially the rupiah, is very firm. Therefore, we have stepped up the intensity of our \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Governor \uc2 Perry Warjiyo\uc2  told reporters.\par \par \uc2 The rupiah has lost nearly 8 percent this year, caught up in an emerging market sell-off, which accelerated this week after a plunge in Argentina's peso followed Turkish lira volatility.\par \par \uc2 Warjiyo said BI will continue to monitor market developments in Argentina and Turkey, though he stressed that Indonesia's economy was "strong" with sound indicators.\par \par \uc2 Indonesia's benchmark 10-year bond yield hit 8.094 percent on Friday, the highest since December 2016, and against a previous closing of 7.967 percent.\par \par \uc2 Nanang Hendarsah, BI's head of monetary management, said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was "decisively" \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the rupiah and halt a fall in bond prices, citing the purchase on Friday of 3 trillion rupiah ($203.74 million) of bonds sold by foreign investors.\par \par \uc2 Bond outflows had pressured the rupiah and so far this year BI had bought around 80 trillion rupiah of government bonds to control rising yields, Hendarsah said.\par \par \uc2 Indonesia's main stock index, which has seen nearly 50 trillion rupiah ($3.40 billion) of net foreign outflows this year, was down 0.7 percent at 0830 GMT, after shedding 1.3 percent earlier on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Asian markets were also under renewed pressure on Friday after a report that U.S. President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was preparing to step up a trade war with Beijing.\par \par \uc2 BI has raised interest rates four times since May by a total of 125 basis points, trying to get investors to buy Indonesian assets again. The next policy meeting is on Sept. 26-27.\par \par \uc2 Satria Sambijantoro, an economist at Bahana Securities, said BI remained the sole supplier of dollars in the onshore \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market as exporters held onto theirs and importers appear to be buying more than they need.\par \par \uc2 The government has unveiled plans to tackle surging imports, which add to a shortage in onshore dollar supply and widening the current account deficit.\par \par \uc2 The plans include higher import tariffs for some consumer goods and enforcing wider use of biodiesel from next month to cut oil imports. Despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakness, Indonesia's annual inflation rate is seen only picking up slightly in August. For the second quarter, Indonesia reported annual economic growth of 5.3 percent, the fastest in 4-1/2 years.\par \par \uc2 ($1 = 14,725 rupiah) (Additional reporting by Cindy Silviana, Tabita Diela and Fransiska Nangoy; Editing by Ed Davies and Richard Borsuk) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2018-8-31T10:00:19.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020180831ee8v00hil\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
