\par\par \b \uc2 SE Asia Stocks-Singapore, Indonesia drop amid China growth fears\b0\par\par\uc2 464 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 August 2018\par 12:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2018 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     * Singapore falls for second straight session\par     * Thailand snaps three-session gaining streak\par \par     By Sumeet Gaikwad\par     Aug 30 (Reuters) - Singapore and Indonesia tracked broader\par Asian shares lower on Thursday as fears of cooling economic\par growth in China amid its trade spat with the United States\par dampened investor sentiment.\par     Activity in China's vast factory sector likely slowed for\par the third straight month in August as domestic demand remained\par weak and exporters faced rising uncertainties from the\par escalating trade war with Washington, a Reuters poll showed.\par \par     The deadline for public comment on U.S. President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Donald\par Trump\f28 \uc2 \f2 's tariffs on another $200 billion of Chinese goods is on\par Sept. 5, with the new measures possibly taking effect next\par month.\par     Indonesian shares closed 0.8 percent lower, paring\par early gains, on broad-based losses as the rupiah continues to\par lose ground.\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign\par exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market to keep the rupiah from depreciating too fast\par and too sharply, an official said on Thursday. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has\par lost about 7 percent this year.\par     The index of the country's 45 most liquid stocks\par was down more than 1 percent. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Telekomunikasi Indonesia\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par fell 2 percent, while \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank Negara Indonesia\f28 \uc2 \f2  declined\par nearly 3 percent.  \par     Singapore shares ended lower for a second consecutive\par session, weighed down by industrials and financials. \par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 United Overseas Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Jardine Matheson Holdings\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par  fell 1.4 percent and 2 percent, respectively. \par     Philippines index climbed to an over three-month\par closing high, on gains in financial stocks. \par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 Metro Pacific Investments Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2  was the biggest\par boost to the benchmark, rising 5.4 percent, while \f28 \uc2 \f2 GT Capital\par Holdings\f28 \uc2 \f2  added 4.5 percent.\par     Malaysian shares were largely flat as losses in\par telecom stocks offset gains in financials and energy.\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 Telekom Malaysia\f28 \uc2 \f2  was the biggest drag on the\par index, sliding more than 7 percent.\par     Thai shares ended marginally lower, snapping a\par three-session gaining streak, weighed down by consumer staples\par and energy stocks. \par \par For Asian Companies click;  \par \par SOUTHEAST ASIAN STOCK MARKETS\par  Change on the day                            \par  Market             Current   Previous close  Pct Move\par  Singapore          3225.72   3243.92         -0.56\par  Bangkok            1720.43   1722.26         -0.11\par  Manila             7853.16   7830.96         0.28\par  Jakarta            6018.964  6065.149        -0.76\par  Kuala Lumpur       1819.66   1820.64         -0.05\par  Ho Chi Minh        998.07    988.17          1.00\par                                               \par  Change on year                               \par  Market             Current   End 2017        Pct Move\par  Singapore          3225.72   3402.92         -5.21\par  Bangkok            1720.43   1753.71         -1.90\par  Manila             7853.16   8558.42         -8.24\par  Jakarta            6018.964  6355.654        -5.30\par  Kuala Lumpur       1819.66   1796.81  