\par\par \b \uc2 New Zealand's Economy at a Delicate Juncture -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,254 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 August 2018\par 04:55\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0255 GMT - The New Zealand economy is at a delicate juncture, says ANZ. Overall structural imbalances are not too bad, but home price/income ratios are well above sustainable levels, it contends. That as fiscal stimulus is the largest in years, the labor market is tight and consumers are still saying it's a great time to buy a major household item. Also, the country's terms of trade is nearly record highs. For ANZ, none of that takes away from the New Zealand remaining vulnerable or being able to shake the feeling that the external environment is as good as it gets. When businesses are feeling worried, the reaction to any bad news--in terms of shelving investment and hiring plans--could be swift. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0249 GMT - \uc2 Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  's alleged \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market today helps send a message that rupiah stability remains its short-term priority, says a dealer with a foreign bank. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is suspected to have sold dollars at around IDR14,600 after the rupiah hit fresh 3-year lows. The pair has steadied around that level after getting to IDR14,648 low earlier in the day. \uc2 Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  usually declines to confirm a specific \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (i-made.sentana@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0233 GMT - If China's budget is fully implemented this year, the fiscal deficit would be 0.9% of GDP more than last year, or about CNY1.1 trillion ($161 billion), Standard Chartered estimates. Actual implementation in 1H pointed to a possible deficit undershooting, suggesting authorities are now under pressure to accelerate implementation through tax cuts and faster infrastructure spending, the investment bank says. That as the firm ticked up its 2018 GDP-growth target to 6.6% from 6.5% amid 1H's strength and what it calls the government's measures to support demand. (liyan.qi@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0230 GMT - As Indonesia's rupiah hits fresh near-3-year lows versus the dollar, some economists say emerging Asian economies like Indonesia's are in strong position to absorb external shocks. \uc2 Natixis\uc2  this morning noted countries have "savings to absorb shock" through means like foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves and can take "decisive steps to ensure macroeconomic stability." The dollar is around IDR14,600, versus 14,437 late Friday. Indonesia's JSX stock benchmark is down 1.8% in early trading, among the region's biggest decliners. (i-made.sentana@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0225 GMT - Countries with large amounts of dollar-denominated debt will face strong pressure near-term from the greenback's gains and ongoing US interest-rate hikes. Apart from Turkey, the risk is there for other emerging-market countries like Malaysia and Indonesia. AmBank Research notes Malaysia's ringgit is fairly well-correlated with the lira at around 79%, the firm estimates that every 1% drop in the lira against the dollar, equates to a 0.04% immediate impact on the ringgit. It's down slightly this morning at about MYR4.09/dollar. But Indonesia's rupiah has slid nearly 1% this morning, continuing to near 2015's record low. (yantoultra.ngui@wsj.com; @yantoultra)\par \par \uc2 0213 GMT - More session highs for the yen and 1%+ declines in Asian stock markets are helping push up prices for Japanese government bonds. Yields for the benchmark 10-year is down a half-basis point at 0.095% while 30s are off 1.5bp at 0.825%. The yen is now widely up 0.7-1.0%. (suryatapa.bhattacharya@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0210 GMT - South Korea's early-August trade data find memory chips leading strong export growth. Through Friday, overall August outbound shipments jumped 19% from a year earlier to $15 billion, which semiconductors surging 41%, customs data show. Smartphone releases and business investment in AI and the Internet of Things have recently driven up demand for memory chips despite some caution that growth may moderate. \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  last week launched a new flagship smartphone devise, Galaxy Note 9 and separately announced a $22 billion investment plan to nurture growth engines including AI. (kwanwoo.jun@wsj.com; @kwanwoo)\par \par \uc2 0203 GMT - [Dow Jones ] Standard Chartered expects the Chinese yuan to weaken to CNY6.92 versus the dollar by the end of 2018 from CNY6.58 seen earlier. The bank also sees USD/CNY above 7.0 in the first half of 2019 as yuan weakness is tolerated by Beijing as a means of buffering the economy. "We expect economic divergence between the U.S. and China, and pessimism about the U.S.-China trade war to weigh on the CNY," the bank says. But any weakness is expected to be moderate given positive market dynamics relative to 2015-16. "Foreign investors continue to add to China asset holdings and authorities are unlikely to discourage this behaviour," it says. (kenan.machado@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0201 GMT - It may not be long before the ongoing downturn in Australian home prices surpasses the average decline of 4.8%, says \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  . The latest drop in the home sales/listing ratio suggests that by year-end prices could be falling at a 5% annual rate. A 3% drop from August-December would result in a 5.3% fall from the peak, more than 4 of the prior 7 downturns and not far behind the 5.7% decrease seen ahead of the 1990-1 recession, Australia's last. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0200 GMT - [Dow Jones ] An ability to absorb the shock of "discriminating capital" should help emerging markets in Asia withstand shocks better than countries such as Turkey, says \uc2 Natixis\uc2  . EMs in the region have ample \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves relative to short-term external debt, prudent fiscal and monetary policy to prevent overheating and take decisive steps to ensure macroeconomic stability, the French investment bank says. Even Asian EMs such as Philippines, Indonesia and India that have current account deficits have kept the deficits below 2% of GDP, \uc2 Natixis\uc2  says. In comparison, Turkey has a current account deficit worth 6.3% of GDP. Asian central banks are also decisive to move if required to ensure price stability, \uc2 Natixis\uc2  said.(kenan.machado@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0143 GMT - While New Zealand's service sector saw bigger expansion last month, results were mixed in the details. The BNZ - BusinessNZ Performance of Services Index advanced to 55.1 from June's 52.7, but that month's slump wasn't fully reversed. BusinessNZ CEO \uc2 Kirk Hope\uc2  says while it was good to see a rebound last month, there were still elements of the July result that gives pause for thought. Although key subindices of activity/sales and new orders recovered somewhat, others remained sluggish. That as employment fell into contraction territory for the first time since January 2013. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0126 GMT - The Singapore dollar has fallen further against the greenback this morning, as the US \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength in the wake of Turkey's woes drives the market. Despite Singapore reiterating 2018 economic-growth forecasts this morning, its dollar fell following the release of updated GDP data, with 2Q's growth a bit less than previously stated. The greenback is at S$1.3760, hitting fresh 1-year lows, versus S$1.3738 immediately before the GDP release, and it's rebounded some 5% since April. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires