\par\par \b \uc2 Indian Morning Briefing: Asian Markets Broadly Lower on Turkey Worries\b0\par\par\uc2 1,207 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 August 2018\par 05:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GLOBAL MARKETS \par DJIA         25313.14  -196.09  -0.77% \par Nasdaq         7839.11   -52.67  -0.67% \par S&P 500       2833.28   -20.30  -0.71% \par FTSE 100      7667.01   -74.76  -0.97% \par Nikkei Stock 21929.70  -368.38  -1.65% \par Hang Seng    27880.54  -486.08  -1.71% \par Kospi         2251.33   -31.46  -1.38% \par SGX Nifty    11380.00   -51.50  -0.45% \par Aug contract \par  \par USD/JPY 110.19-20  -0.63% \par Range   110.90   110.15 \par EUR/USD 1.1376-79  -0.30% \par Range   1.1412   1.1369 \par  \par CBOT  Wheat Sept $5.466 per bushel \par Comex Gold    $1211.10   -0.07% \par Nymex  Crude (NY) $67.73  +0.92 \par  \par  \par U.S. STOCKS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The S&P 500 and Dow Jones Industrial Average fell, snapping a five-week streak of gains, as renewed trade tensions and a slide in the Turkish lira rippled across global markets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 Trading had been relatively quiet for much of the week with the corporate earnings season winding down, keeping major indexes in a relatively narrow range through Thursday.\par \par \uc2 But stocks around the world lost traction Friday as selling accelerated in the Turkish lira, sending the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to a fresh low against the dollar and raising fears that the country may be unable to repay debt denominated in other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Dow industrials slumped 196.09 points, or 0.8%, to 25313.14. The S&P 500 slipped 20.30 points, or 0.7%, to 2833.28 sliding further from its January high, and the technology-heavy Nasdaq Composite dropped 52.67 points, or 0.7%, to 7839.11, breaking an eight-session winning streak.\par \par \uc2 For the week, the Dow industrials dropped 0.6%, while the S&P 500 posted a 0.2% loss and the Nasdaq advanced 0.3%.\par \par \uc2 Although markets wobbled Friday, many investors and analysts remain optimistic about the nine-year bull market. With results in from 91% of companies, the S&P 500 is on track to report the second fastest pace of earnings growth since the third quarter of 2010, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Away from the trade war, just about everything is lining up positively," said \uc2 Julian Emanuel\uc2  , chief equity and derivatives strategist at \uc2 BTIG\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, a measure of expected stock swings climbed. The \uc2 Cboe\uc2  Volatility Index, sometimes referred to as Wall Street's "fear gauge," jumped 17% in its biggest one-day percentage climb since June. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ASIAN STOCKS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 After muted reaction Friday afternoon as Turkey's lira began its plunge, Asian stocks are catching up today following stock declines overseas to end the week. Many indexes are down more than 1% as midday nears, with Down Under the noted outperformer with drops of about 0.5% in Australia and New Zealand. Moves are pretty uniform, with Indonesia modestly outpacing with a 2% decline in the JSX index as the country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  falls some 1% versus the dollar. Declines have also built in derivatives, with S&P 500 futures now down 0.35%. \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 The lira sank to a record low on Sunday evening after collapsing last week. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is now down more than 40% this year, while bond yields have skyrocketed, pushing Turkey onto the edge of a financial crisis. Turkey's vulnerabilities include high levels of foreign-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  debt, a current-account deficit and rising borrowing costs.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Countries with large amounts of dollar-denominated debt will face strong pressure near-term from the greenback's gains and ongoing U.S. interest-rate hikes. Apart from Turkey, the risk is there for other emerging-market countries like Malaysia and Indonesia. AmBank Research notes Malaysia's ringgit is fairly well-correlated with the lira at around 79%, the firm estimates that every 1% drop in the lira against the dollar, equates to a 0.04% immediate impact on the ringgit. It's down slightly this morning at about MYR4.09/dollar. But Indonesia's rupiah has slid nearly 1% this morning, continuing to near 2015's record low.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Standard Chartered\f28 \uc2 \f2  expects the Chinese yuan to weaken to CNY6.92 versus the dollar by the end of 2018 from CNY6.58 seen earlier. The bank sees USD/CNY above 7.0 in the first half of 2019 as yuan weakness is tolerated by Beijing as a means of buffering the economy. "We expect economic divergence between the U.S. and China, and pessimism about the U.S.-China trade war to weigh on the CNY," the bank said. But any weakness is expected to be moderate given positive market dynamics relative to 2015-16. "Foreign investors continue to add to China asset holdings and authorities are unlikely to discourage this behaviour," it said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  's alleged \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market today helped send a message that rupiah stability remains its short-term priority, said a dealer with a foreign bank. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is suspected to have sold dollars at around IDR14,600 after the rupiah hit fresh 3-year lows. The pair has steadied around that level after getting to IDR14,648 low earlier in the day. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  usually declines to confirm a specific \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par METALS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 London spot-gold prices edged lower in Asian trading as the dollar remained strong amid the Turkey crisis. The metal, which remains near a 1-year low, should be attracting some investment buying at these levels, said Stuart Ive at OM Financial, adding that current market uncertainty should eventually benefit gold. However, for months now safe-haven flows have been moving more to the dollar. Spot gold was recently down 0.1% at $1,209/troy ounce. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par OIL SUMMARY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Oil futures were modestly higher in early Asian trading, building on Friday's gains in the wake of Turkey's travails. The lira has skidded anew this morning, with the dollar up nearly 6% at above TRY6.8 after earlier breaching TRY7 for the first time. Highlighting the speed of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's plunge, the dollar in Thursday morning Asian action was at TRY5.3. Oil on Friday was also helped by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 IEA\f28 \uc2 \f2  boosting projected global oil demand for next year. Robust buying of crude has been key in keeping the market up in the face of looming supply increases from Saudi Arabia and Russia. September WTI was recently up 0.3% at $67.85/barrel and October Brent was 0.2% higher to $72.96. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES \par  \par Turkish Lira's Fall Raises Concerns for Emerging-Market Investors \par Erdogan Faces Crossroads in Dispute With U.S. \par Chinese Banks Rev Up Lending to Buoy Economy in U.S. Trade Fight \par Abe's Window of Time for Amending Japan's Pacifist Constitution Narrows \par Google Woos Partners for Potential China Expansion \par Optimism on European Growth Reverses as Headwinds Mount \par U.S. Takes Trade Fight to Smaller Nations \par Trump Poses Wild Card in Farm Bill Negotiations \par Frackers Burn Cash to Sustain U.S. Oil Boom \par Australian Pipeline Company's Directors Approve Consortium's Bid \par Australian Steelmaker Mulls $700 Million U.S. Investment \par Bridgepoint to Sell Minority Stake to Neuberger Berman  Unit \par Wrangler Owner Looks to Exit Jeans Business \par Caspian Nations Move to Settle Dispute on Oil and Gas Reserves \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 August 12, 2018 23:00 ET (03:00 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020180813ee8d0004t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
