\par\par \b \uc2 Hong Kong GDP Growth Seen Easing Further in 2H -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,179 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 August 2018\par 05:52\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0352 GMT - After Hong Kong GDP grew slower-than-expected 3.5% in 2Q, \uc2 OCBC\uc2  is among those anticipating further deceleration in 2H for the city's export-oriented economy. But beyond trade, which could hit Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and weighing on Hong Kong's tourism, it could also pressure local equities--and hence consumer sentiment. The investment bank says 2018's GDP growth could come in at 3.2%, versus last year's 3.8%, if trade tensions escalate. (chester.yung@wsj.com; @chester_yung)\par \par \uc2 0336 GMT - RBA Gov. Lowe has stressed over the last year that should wage growth not improve, the bank's inflation targets probably won't be achieved. That helps make Wednesday's 2Q wage reading notable. \uc2 RBC\uc2  see little reason for increases to deviate much from the recent quarterly pace of 0.5%. Just 2% annually is the kind of result that will have the chief central banker slumping back into his chair. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0311 GMT - The New Zealand and Australian dollars are respectively 6% and 2% below \uc2 NAB\uc2  's short-term fair-value estimates, suggesting growing discounts which reflect a more-uncertain global outlook. The investment bank expects to see another lurch down in the duo on any fresh tariffs of the scale proposed by the White House, meaning the kiwi falling below US$0.65 and the Aussie US$0.70 can't be ruled out. However, a de-escalation of trade tensions could generate modest recoveries in both \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0305 GMT - The upticks in Hong Kong mortgage rates "is in line with our view that financial conditions will be gradually tightened" there as local interest rates "eventually catch up with" those in the US, says \uc2 Nomura\uc2 . It helps reiterate the bank's call of GDP rising just 2.3% next year, versus 2018's projected 4%, "as we believe the burden of mortgage-debt servicing and a modest housing-market correction should lower consumption growth." (john.wu@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0255 GMT - The New Zealand economy is at a delicate juncture, says ANZ. Overall structural imbalances are not too bad, but home price/income ratios are well above sustainable levels, it contends. That as fiscal stimulus is the largest in years, the labor market is tight and consumers are still saying it's a great time to buy a major household item. Also, the country's terms of trade is nearly record highs. For ANZ, none of that takes away from the New Zealand remaining vulnerable or being able to shake the feeling that the external environment is as good as it gets. When businesses are feeling worried, the reaction to any bad news--in terms of shelving investment and hiring plans--could be swift. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 0249 GMT - \uc2 Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  's alleged \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market today helps send a message that rupiah stability remains its short-term priority, says a dealer with a foreign bank. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is suspected to have sold dollars at around IDR14,600 after the rupiah hit fresh 3-year lows. The pair has steadied around that level after getting to IDR14,648 low earlier in the day. \uc2 Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  usually declines to confirm a specific \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (i-made.sentana@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0233 GMT - If China's budget is fully implemented this year, the fiscal deficit would be 0.9% of GDP more than last year, or about CNY1.1 trillion ($161 billion), Standard Chartered estimates. Actual implementation in 1H pointed to a possible deficit undershooting, suggesting authorities are now under pressure to accelerate implementation through tax cuts and faster infrastructure spending, the investment bank says. That as the firm ticked up its 2018 GDP-growth target to 6.6% from 6.5% amid 1H's strength and what it calls the government's measures to support demand. (liyan.qi@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0230 GMT - As Indonesia's rupiah hits fresh near-3-year lows versus the dollar, some economists say emerging Asian economies like Indonesia's are in strong position to absorb external shocks. \uc2 Natixis\uc2  this morning noted countries have "savings to absorb shock" through means like foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves and can take "decisive steps to ensure macroeconomic stability." The dollar is around IDR14,600, versus 14,437 late Friday. Indonesia's JSX stock benchmark is down 1.8% in early trading, among the region's biggest decliners. (i-made.sentana@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0225 GMT - Countries with large amounts of dollar-denominated debt will face strong pressure near-term from the greenback's gains and ongoing US interest-rate hikes. Apart from Turkey, the risk is there for other emerging-market countries like Malaysia and Indonesia. AmBank Research notes Malaysia's ringgit is fairly well-correlated with the lira at around 79%, the firm estimates that every 1% drop in the lira against the dollar, equates to a 0.04% immediate impact on the ringgit. It's down slightly this morning at about MYR4.09/dollar. But Indonesia's rupiah has slid nearly 1% this morning, continuing to near 2015's record low. (yantoultra.ngui@wsj.com; @yantoultra)\par \par \uc2 0213 GMT - More session highs for the yen and 1%+ declines in Asian stock markets are helping push up prices for Japanese government bonds. Yields for the benchmark 10-year is down a half-basis point at 0.095% while 30s are off 1.5bp at 0.825%. The yen is now widely up 0.7-1.0%. (suryatapa.bhattacharya@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0210 GMT - South Korea's early-August trade data find memory chips leading strong export growth. Through Friday, overall August outbound shipments jumped 19% from a year earlier to $15 billion, which semiconductors surging 41%, customs data show. Smartphone releases and business investment in AI and the Internet of Things have recently driven up demand for memory chips despite some caution that growth may moderate. \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  last week launched a new flagship smartphone devise, Galaxy Note 9 and separately announced a $22 billion investment plan to nurture growth engines including AI. (kwanwoo.jun@wsj.com; @kwanwoo)\par \par \uc2 0203 GMT - [Dow Jones ] Standard Chartered expects the Chinese yuan to weaken to CNY6.92 versus the dollar by the end of 2018 from CNY6.58 seen earlier. The bank also sees USD/CNY above 7.0 in the first half of 2019 as yuan weakness is tolerated by Beijing as a means of buffering the economy. "We expect economic divergence between the U.S. and China, and pessimism about the U.S.-China trade war to weigh on the CNY," the bank says. But any weakness is expected to be moderate given positive market dynamics relative to 2015-16. "Foreign investors continue to add to China asset holdings and authorities are unlikely to discourage this behaviour," it says. (kenan.machado@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 12, 2018 23:52 ET (03:52 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020180813ee8d0002s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
