\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Lira crisis hits Asian  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  as investors flee to safety\b0\par\par\uc2 786 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 August 2018\par 08:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2018 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  (Adds text, updates prices)\par     By Chandini Monnappa\par     Aug 13 (Reuters) - Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  were battered on Monday\par with the Indian rupee touching a record low as the ongoing\par crisis in Turkey spilled over to emerging markets and investors\par flocked to safe-haven assets.\par     The Turkish lira tumbled to a record low,\par weakening as much as 13.34 percent, over concerns about Turkish\par President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Tayyip Erdogan\f28 \uc2 \f2 's rigid control over the economy and a\par worsening diplomatic rift with the United States.\par     "If investors continue to be concerned about the events in\par Turkey, and they likely will be, we could see more pain in store\par when it comes to risk appetite in the week ahead," Jameel Ahmad,\par global head of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategy & market research at FXTM wrote\par in a note.\par     Investors fear the sell-off in the lira could have a ripple\par effect in global financial markets with the euro, the South\par African rand and Mexico's peso already on the receiving end from\par Turkey's crisis.\par     Erdogan, who has called himself the "enemy of interest\par rates," wants cheap credit from banks to fuel growth, but\par investors fear the economy is overheating and could be set for a\par hard landing.\par     Worries about the exposure of European banks to crisis-hit\par Turkey prompted investors to bid up safe havens such as the U.S.\par dollar and Japanese yen.\par     The dollar index added 0.07 percent at 96.423 while\par the Yen strengthened 0.58 percent by 0535 GMT\par     "Today's market is a little bit more about recalibrating the\par investment agenda. Investors want to reduce their position. Kind\par of like, once bitten, twice shy," said Taye Shim, head of\par research at Jakarta-based Mirae Asset Sekuritas.\par     The Indonesian rupiah plunged nearly 1 percent on to\par its weakest since October 2015, after data showed on Friday the\par country's current account deficit, a major concern for global\par emerging market investors, swelled in the second quarter to the\par largest in nearly four years.\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to defend the\par rupiah, a senior official said on Monday.\par     "The bank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in both the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and bond market can\par only smooth excessive movement in the rupiah not change its\par direction," said Gao Qi, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Strategist(EM Asia)at Scotiabank.\par     China's yuan slid 0.5 percent, in line with\par regional peers.\par     "A worsening yuan is like pouring oil on a fire," said\par Mirae's Shim. \par      \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China (PBOC) \f28 \uc2 \f2 lowered the yuan midpoint\par rate to 6.8629 per dollar, its weakest level since\par May 31, 2017, which was 0.3 percent softer than Friday's fix of\par 6.8395.\par     Last week, the yuan fell for the ninth straight week, the\par longest weekly losing streak since 1994. It has fallen about 9\par percent against the dollar since the end of March.\par     South Korea's won and the Taiwan dollar\par weakened 0.48 percent and 0.32 percent respectively.\par     \par     THE INDIAN RUPEE\par  \par     India's rupee, which has been the region's worst\par performing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  so far this year, weakened more than one\par percent hours before consumer inflation data for July was due.\par     A Reuters poll found that July inflation was expected to be \par 4.51 percent versus a five-month high of 5.0 percent hit in\par June.\par     Inflation remains a central concern for India, with rising \par crude oil prices adding to imported inflation. Moreover, recent\par erratic Indian monsoons have dimmed the outlook for\par winter-harvested crops in a largely agriculture-reliant economy.\par       \par     \par     The following table shows rates for Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against\par the dollar at 0600 GMT.\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR\par     \par     \par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                      Latest bid  Previous day  Pct Move\par  Japan yen                    110.240     110.92        +0.62\par  Sing dlr                     1.376       1.3733        -0.18\par  Taiwan dlr                   30.807      30.708        -0.32\par  Korean won                   1134.400    1128.9        -0.48\par  Peso                         53.330      53.135        -0.37\par  Rupiah                       14610.000   14470         -0.96\par  Rupee                        69.455      68.83         -0.91\par  Ringgit                      4.090       4.083         -0.17\par  Yuan                         6.875       6.8440        -0.46\par                                                         \par  Change so far in 2018                                  \par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                      Latest bid  End 2017      Pct Move\par  Japan yen                    110.240     112.67        +2.20\par  Sing dlr                     1.376       1.3373        -2.80\par  Tai