\par\par \b \uc2 SE Asia Stocks-Indonesian shares plunge amid rout in EM  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2 , stocks\b0\par\par\uc2 568 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 August 2018\par 06:42\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2018 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     * Indonesia posts worst pct drop since Nov 2016\par     * Philippines marks biggest pct decline in over 7 weeks\par     * Singapore, Malaysia slide more than 1 pct\par \par     By Karthika Suresh Namboothiri\par     Aug 13 (Reuters) - Indonesian shares plummeted on Monday in\par their worst drop in nearly two years as emerging market\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  faced a deep selloff, causing a market rout across\par Southeast Asia.  \par     The Turkish lira and the Indian rupee plunged to all-time\par lows on Monday, while the Indonesian rupiah slid to a near\par three-year low.\par     "Emerging markets have very weak current account balance\par which would have very negative impact," said Taye Shim, head of\par research at Jakarta-based Mirae Asset Sekuritas.\par     "That is prompting investors to reassess their investment\par thesis on emerging markets as we really don't know how it is\par going to play out."\par     The lira has lost about 45 percent of its value\par against the dollar this year, largely over worries about\par President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Tayyip Erdogan\f28 \uc2 \f2 's influence over the economy, his\par repeated calls for lower interest rates in the face of high\par inflation and deteriorating ties with the United States.\par \par     The lira's drop unnerved global investors, with MSCI's\par broadest index of Asia-Pacific shares outside Japan\par  shedding as much as 1.6 percent on Monday.\par     Indonesian shares slumped more than 3 percent, marking their\par steepest intraday percentage drop since November 2016.\par     The rupiah also plunged 0.9 percent on Monday to its weakest\par since October 2015, after data showed on Friday the country's\par current account deficit, a major concern for global emerging\par market investors, swelled to the largest in nearly four years in\par the second quarter. \par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to defend the\par rupiah, a senior official said on Monday.\par     Financials led the losses in the stocks, with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank Central\par Asia\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank Mandiri\f28 \uc2 \f2  sliding as much as 3.5\par percent and 6.5 percent, respectively.\par     The index of the country's 45 most liquid stocks\par dropped nearly 4 percent.\par     Philippines also plunged, by 2 percent, marking its worst\par drop since June 22.\par     SM investments Corp dropped over 2.7 percent, while\par real estate developer Ayala Land fell 3.6 percent.\par     Singapore shares dropped 1.4 percent to their lowest\par in nearly four weeks, dragged down largely by financials. \par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 Oversea-Chinese Banking Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2  fell as much as 2.3\par percent while \f28 \uc2 \f2 DBS Group Holdings\f28 \uc2 \f2  hit a five-week low.\par     Singapore's economy grew slower than initial estimates in\par the April-June period on a quarter-on-quarter basis, revised\par data showed on Monday, as the government flagged a likely\par moderation in growth in the second half.\par     Thailand was closed for a holiday.\par     \par For Asian Companies click;  \par \par SOUTHEAST ASIAN STOCK MARKETS AS AT 0436 GMT\par  Market          Current   Previous close  Pct Move\par  Singapore       3245.55   3284.78         -1.19\par  Manila          7651.99   7804.98         -1.96\par  Jakarta         5885.542  6077.173        -3.15\par  Kuala Lumpur    1785.27   1805.75         -1.13\par  Ho Chi Minh     970      