\par\par \b \uc2 Indonesia's  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  reserves decline $1.5 bln in July - c.bank\b0\par\par\uc2 204 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 August 2018\par 13:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2018 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA, Aug 7 (Reuters) - Indonesia's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves fell $1.5 billion in July to $118.3 billion, partly because of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the rupiah, \uc2 Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BI) \uc2 said on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 The end-July reserves level was equal to 6.9 months of imports, above the international adequacy standard of three months, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 BI said reserves were used in July to defend the rupiah "amid high uncertainty in the global financial market" and pay for the government's external debt.\par \par \uc2 At the end of January, Indonesia's reserves were $132 billion. They have declined $13.7 billion from February through July, as BI stabilized the rupiah amid an emerging market sell-off triggered by rising United States interest rates and a brewing trade war that is impacting global growth.\par \par \uc2 The rupiah has lost more than 6 percent of value against the dollar this year.\par \par \uc2 Between mid-May and end-June, BI raised its key interest rate by 100 basis points to 5.25 percent. (Reporting by Nilufar Rizki and Gayatri Suroyo; Editing by Richard Borsuk) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2018-8-7T12:03:39.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020180807ee8701lsl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw442\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6630\pichgoal600
