\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1- \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia \highlight\uc2  c.bank says  \highlight22\uc2 intervening \highlight\uc2  to support rupiah, preparing to adjust rates\b0\par\par\uc2 480 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Mai 2018\par 17:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2018 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Adds context, \uc2 IMF\uc2  official, details)\par \par \uc2 By Gayatri Suroyo and Maikel Jefriando\par \par \uc2 JAKARTA, May 9 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Wednesday it was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the markets to support the rupiah and was preparing to adjust its benchmark interest rate, suggesting it may hike rates to shore up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , now at its weakest in 2-1/2 years.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, \uc2 Bank Indonesia (BI) \uc2 said it was "in the middle of preparing firm and consistent monetary policy steps including by adjusting the 7-day reverse repo rate", its key rate.\par \par \uc2 BI said it was also "optimising other monetary \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  instruments" to ensure enough rupiah and dollar onshore liquidity.\par \par \uc2 BI has kept its benchmark rate steady since cutting it by 25 basis points last September. But last month, Governor \uc2 Agus Martowardojo\uc2  said he would be ready to raise the key rate if the rupiah's weakness threatened its inflation target or financial stability.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, a deputy governor said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will assess the level of inflation and trade balance as well as global capital flows and U.S. interest rates before deciding whether to hike.\par \par \uc2 At \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2 's May 16-17 policy meeting, "if it's needed and is shown by those data that we have to raise the rate, then we will make an adjustment," deputy governor Mirza Adityaswara said. This week also saw data that showed the Indonesian economy grew at a weaker-than-expected pace of 5.06 percent in the first quarter, adding another headwind to markets already worried about outflows from the high-yielding rupiah bond markets.\par \par \uc2 BI said it was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the rupiah in both the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and bond markets. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was trading on Wednesday at its weakest against the dollar since December 2015.\par \par \uc2 Finance Minister \uc2 Sri Mulyani Indrawati\uc2  said the government currently had enough cash on hand and that it could look for other ways to raise money if investors continue to ask for high yields in future bond auctions.\par \par \uc2 The yield on the benchmark 10-year government bond hit 7.316 percent on Wednesday, the highest since March 2017, as investors sold emerging market assets.\par \par \uc2 In Hong Kong, \uc2 Changyong Rhee\uc2 , director of the International Monetary Fund's Asia-Pacific department, said he was not too worried about recent selling pressure on assets in Indonesia and the Philippines as it was not triggered by domestic factors and Asia has stronger buffers than in the past.\par \par \uc2 "There is room for interest rates in Asia to also go up and also room to rely on the flexible exchange rate system more compared with the period in 1997," he said, adding though that it was "time to watch things closely". ( Writing by Ed Davies Editing by Hugh Lawson) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2018-5-9T16:01:22.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020180509ee5900kut\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
