\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-As  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  weaken, Asia's original sinners go for classic defence\b0\par\par\uc2 886 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Mai 2018\par 07:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2018 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * Higher U.S. rates, trade tensions pressure some Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * Central banks revert to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * Defending \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  without raising rates difficult-strategists\par \par \uc2 * Some countries still seen dependent on foreign portfolio flows\par \par \uc2 By Vidya Ranganathan\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, May 4 (Reuters) - To deal with a familiar combination of falling \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and uncertainty about economic growth, Asian central banks are reverting to their classic policy playbook. They are \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets while concurrently injecting cash into their economies.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Asia's economies with relatively high interest rates, such as Indonesia, India and the Philippines, are each down about 3 percent this year because they are facing a series of challenges, including competition for funds from rising \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yields, a resurgent U.S. dollar, and the rising tensions over global trade and capital outflows as U.S. President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  pushes his \u8220\'20\'1CAmerica first\u8221\'20\'1D agenda.\par \par \uc2 As well as the risk that trade tensions could disrupt the region's supply chains, these economies are already dealing with some slackness in the export orders that had lifted growth in the past year.\par \par \uc2 In Indonesia, the rupiah has fallen 5 percent in three months. \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  has persistently sold dollars to put a floor under its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . At the same time, it is buying bonds to replenish the rupiah it has absorbed through that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 While \uc2 Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  says it is willing to raise rates if need be to defend the rupiah, analysts suspect it is loath to do so.\par \par \uc2 "This is all very well, until it doesn't necessarily work," said Claudio Piron, a strategist with Bank of America Merrill Lynch in Singapore.\par \par \uc2 Trying to stabilise \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  while resisting the need to raise interest rates is a challenge, he said.\par \par \uc2 "What happens if the domestic liquidity that you are generating just exits the country and causes the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to depreciate? When you reach that point, then maybe you need to raise rates to try and attract portfolio inflows again."\par \par \uc2 FIRST LINE OF DEFENCE\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  by buying and selling \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been the default policy option since the 1997/98 financial crisis for Asia's governments.\par \par \uc2 Even now, rather than raise interest rates in lockstep with the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  , they are selling dollars and supplanting the easy money that global central banks are slowly removing from the world economy.\par \par \uc2 A big part of the reason has been the historic and high dependence on foreign portfolio flows, often referred to as the 'original sin' in the markets.\par \par \uc2 Despite paring a big chunk of their holdings in the past 6 to 8 months, foreigners still hold 40 percent of Indonesian government bonds and 45 percent of Malaysia's. They are also heavily invested in Thailand.\par \par \uc2 That means their monetary policy is hostage to external market conditions and these central banks have to strike a balance between keeping their economies growing solidly and their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  stable. Yields have to be high enough to lure foreign investors but stable too, so that there isn't a bond selloff.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CCapital flows into Asia not only because of interest rate differentials, but it\u8217\'20\'19s also about the growth prospects and the investment returns including those in real projects," said Frances Cheung, head of Asia macro strategy at \uc2 Westpac\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CFor Indonesia in particular, we think raising interest rates should be the last option."\par \par \uc2 While Malaysia and Thailand can count on their local pension funds and state institutions to support the bond market in a crisis, that support isn\u8217\'20\'19t as clear cut in India and Indonesia.\par \par \uc2 India is therefore liberalizing bond investment rules to entice more foreigners.\par \par \uc2 Analysts expect that even South Korea, whose \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has the buffer of a huge current account surplus, will release funds into the market by going easy on bond issuance in the latter half of the year, just to compensate for the foreign capital outflows.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has said it is content to leave monetary policy as is, despite rising inflation, a weak peso and falling stock market. Worried about a slowdown in the overseas workers' remittances back to the country, it even cut banks' reserve ratio early this year.\par \par \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  spent about $6 billion of its reserves in February-March to defend the rupiah. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has over the past couple of weeks stabilised around 13,950 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's bond buying \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  has also put a lid on yields, with the three-month cost of shorting the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  versus the dollar steady around 5.25 percent, the same as levels through 2017.\par \par \uc2 "If global rates continue rising, \uc2 Bank Indonesia\uc2  will not have much choice but to follow suit or risk further outflows," said one foreign investor in Indonesian dollar bonds.\par \par \uc2 Analysts however reckon the absence of inflationary pressures will allow Indonesia and others to continue this kind of policy for a while.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey have bought themselves a bit of time," said Piron at Bank of America Merrill Lynch . "But if they lose 14,000 and if they sustain drawdowns of $5 billion of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves a month, then I think that strategy is going to be harder and harder."\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Fransiska Nangoy and Maikel Jefriando in JAKARTA) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2018-5-4T06:00:00.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020180504ee54007kp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
