\par\par \b \uc2 POLL-Positions on Asian  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  pull back on resurgent dollar\b0\par\par\uc2 946 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Mai 2018\par 08:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2018 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     * Positions on most regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pull back\par     * Bullish bets rise for S. Korean won, Malaysian ringgit \par     * Chinese yuan's bullish bets at lowest in 6 months\par \par     By Ambar Warrick\par     May 3 (Reuters) - Investors trimmed their positions on most\par emerging Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the last two weeks, a Reuters poll\par showed, following recent strength in the dollar on signs of\par resilience in the U.S. economy and a rise in Treasury yields.\par     U.S. debt yields surged over the past two weeks on a\par sell-off in the bond market, while robust economic data in the\par United States, pulled the rug out from under riskier asset\par classes.\par     The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  on Wednesday indicated that inflation\par was nearing the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's target, and that it remained on\par track to raise borrowing costs in June.\par     As such, the dollar gained against a basket of\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , underpinned by the Fed's upward path for its\par benchmark interest rate.\par     \par     ASIAN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  TO RETREAT \par     A stronger dollar bodes poorly for Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , as it\par will lure capital from the region's volatile markets to\par relatively safer assets in the United States.\par     A poll of 12 analysts reflected the shift in portfolios, as\par positions on most Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  were pulled back.\par     Bearish bets on the Indian rupee surged to their\par highest since August 2013, reflecting recent concerns about\par sustainable growth in the country.\par      India's infrastructure growth slowed to a three-year low of\par 4.2 percent in the fiscal year ending in March, indicating Prime\par Minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Narendra Modi\f28 \uc2 \f2  faces a tough challenge to boost\par investment ahead of general elections due early next year.\par \par     Bullish bets on the Singapore dollar fell to their\par lowest since November 2017. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  shed about 1.1 percent\par to the dollar in April, its worst month since November 2016.\par     The city-state's fundamentals remain strong, however, with\par factory output growing more than expected in March while\par Singapore's Prime Minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Lee Hsien Loong\f28 \uc2 \f2  painted a rosier\par picture of economic performance for 2018 this week.\par     Positions on the Taiwan dollar turned bearish after\par more than nine months, cementing a recent downturn in the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     The Taiwan dollar saw April as its worst month in more than\par two years, and is poised to lose in May as well. \par     An expected slowdown in global tech demand has also fuelled \par speculation about whether recent strong growth in the technology\par sector can be sustained, as Taiwan's GDP growth slowed in the\par first quarter of 2018.\par     Bearish bets on Indonesia's rupiah surged to their\par highest since November 2016, as it bore the brunt of a yield\par prompted sell-off. The rupiah is subject to a large amount of\par foreign exposure, with data from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank Indonesia\f28 \uc2 \f2  showing that\par foreigners held about 39 percent of Indonesian government bonds\par as at the end of March.\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Indonesia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has said it would \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the\par market if volatility in the rupiah got out of hand. \par     \par     EASING TENSIONS BENEFIT WON\par     On the other hand, bullish bets on the South Korean won\par  rose, as easing political tensions with its Northern\par neighbour reaffirmed confidence in the country. \par     The leaders of North and South Korea signed a declaration\par last week agreeing to work for the "complete denuclearisation of\par the Korean peninsula," a pledge of peace following about six\par decades of conflict.\par     The news comes as a great relief for South Korean markets,\par which were rattled by escalated tensions following a barrage of\par missile tests by North Korea last year.\par     The Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  positioning poll is focused on what\par analysts and fund managers believe are the current market\par positions in nine Asian emerging market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : the Chinese\par yuan, South Korean won, Singapore dollar, Indonesian rupiah,\par Taiwan dollar, Indian rupee, Philippine peso, Malaysian ringgit\par and the Thai baht.\par     The poll uses estimates of net long or short positions on a\par scale of minus 3 to plus 3. A score of plus 3 indicates the\par market is significantly long U.S. dollars.\par     The figures include positions held through non-deliverable\par forwards (NDFs).\par     The survey findings are provided below (positions\par in U.S. dollar versus each \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ):     \par     \par  DDMM   CNY    KRW    SGD    IDR    TWD    INR    MYR    PHP   THB\par  3/5    -0.23  -0.38  -0.08  0.56   0.04   1.11   -0.24  0.47  -0.4\par  19/4   -0.52  -0.21  -0.44  0.14   -0.28  0.49   -0.19  0.74  -0.36\par  5/4    -0.65  -0.90  -0.50  0.10   -0.66  -0.05  -0.40  0.73  -0.60\par  22/3   -0.61  -0.48  -0.36  0.52   -0.59  0.41   -0.29  1.13  -0.72\par  8/3    -0.61  -0.64  -0.61  0.24   -0.54  0.19   -0.70  1.05  -0.99\par  22/2   -0.61  -0.32  -0.47  0.04   -0.92  0.04   -0.6   1.21  -1.02\par  8/2    -1.25  -0.34  -0.77  -0.1   -0.88  -0.22  -1.02  0.99  -1.1\par  25/1   -1.4   -0.81  -1.45  -0.79  -1.44  -0.71  -1.57  0.51  -1.48\par  11/1   -0.47  -1.06  -0.75  -0.40  -0.88  -0.86  -1.05  0.00  -1.04\par  14/12  -0.25  -1.02  -0.25  0.00   -0.45  -0.17  -1.08  0.38  -1.12\par  30/11  -0.39  -1.34  -0.66  0.00   -0.54  -0.56  -1.16  0.35  -1.16\par  \par     \par  (Reporting by Ambar Warrick in Bengaluru; Additional reporting\par by Aditya Soni; Editing by Jacqueline Wong)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2018-5-3T07:12:55.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020180503ee53007zb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
