\par\par \b \uc2 Indonesia Stocks at 6-Mo Low; Bank Rout Continues -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,354 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 April 2018\par 11:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0904 GMT - A day after logging their worst day in 17 months, Indonesia stocks fared even worse today as banks in the country continued to see their share prices shrivel amid further selling of the rupiah. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been hitting its lowest levels since early 2016's global stock swoon despite \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , raising the prospect of \uc2 Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  boosting rates to curb capital outflows. The JSX ended down 2.8% at 5909.20, a 6-month closing low. Rakyat, the country's biggest bank by assets, fell another 5.1% today, putting the week's swoon at 14%, while No. 2 Mandiri slid 5.2% to put its decline during the past 4 days at 15%. With modest further declines tomorrow, both could log their worst weeks since 2008. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com; @kevinkingsbury)\par \par \uc2 0850 GMT - Germany's economy will gain momentum in the second quarter after a weak first quarter, which was marked by production losses due to a flu epidemic and an unusually high number of holidays, according to DIW Berlin. The catch-up effect will likely result in 0.7% quarterly growth in the second quarter, after an estimated 0.4% growth during the January-March period. Overall, Germany's upswing is losing momentum this year, but growth will continue "because it's benefiting from strong global demand," says DIW economist Ferdinand Fichtner.\par \par \uc2 (andrea.thomas@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0844 GMT - Comparisons are getting tougher for Singapore manufacturing data. Industrial output jumped 9.8% from a year earlier in 1Q from 4Q's 5.8% growth, notes \uc2 UOB\uc2 . While the numbers reflect "continued resilience in the manufacturing sector," the investment bank says many sectors will start showing deceleration, especially electronics. \uc2 UOB\uc2  continues to expect 4.4% overall industrial-output growth for this year; it jumped 10.5% in 2017. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0818 GMT - Several factors would appear to allow the \uc2 Riksbank\uc2  to start turning away from its loose monetary policy, but these factors won't be enough for the \uc2 Riksbank\uc2  to change stance, says \uc2 Nordea\uc2  chief analyst Andreas Wallstroem says. These factors include a continuing global upswing and rising interest rates accordingly; and in Sweden high resource utilization and inflation remaining close to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's target. "Inflation has most likely peaked and we forecast inflation to undershoot the target in both 2018 and 2019," Mr. Wallstroem says. \uc2 Nordea\uc2  forecasts that the \uc2 Riksbank\uc2  will lift its policy rate in the fourth quarter of 2019, the same time as when it expects a first rise by the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  . (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 0749 GMT - The Swedish krona falls after the \uc2 Riksbank\uc2  expressed concerns over underlying inflation in its monetary policy decision, as it left interest rates unchanged at -0.5%. "Underlying inflation...has been unexpectedly low recently, which raises questions regarding the strength of the development in inflation," the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  says in a statement. Headline inflation rose to 2% in March but that was due to an increase in the oil price. EUR/SEK rises to 10.4401, up 0.25% on the day and up from 10.3945 before the announcement. \uc2 Riksbank\uc2  said the first policy rate increase would come "towards the end of the year, which is somewhat later than previously forecast," although this had been largely expected by investors. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 0742 GMT - Swedish government bond yields are stable after the \uc2 Riksbank\uc2  left its repo rate unchanged at -0.50%, in line with market expectations. The two-year bond yield trades at -0.49%, while the 10-year bond yield trades at 0.77%, same as before the rate decision, according to \uc2 Tradeweb\uc2  data. The \uc2 Riksbank\uc2  also says a rate increase isn't expected until toward the end of the year, while government bond redemptions and coupon payments will be reinvested in the bond portfolio until further notice. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 0721 GMT - The \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  's approach to policy normalization will be "gradual and cautious" in the foreseeable future and the end of net QE purchases will mark the first step in the process, says Silvia Dall'Angelo, senior economist at \uc2 Hermes Investment Management\uc2 . This will be followed by a rate increase as well as a gradual and passive balance-sheet adjustment, she says. Some details on the QE exit remain unknown but as it draws nearer, the monetary policy framework must be reshaped before further steps are taken toward policy normalization, Dall'Angelo says. \uc2 Hermes Investment Management\uc2  expects QE to continue over the fourth quarter with a monthly purchasing pace of between EUR10 billion and EUR15 billion. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 0723 GMT - Mongolian companies need a strong market to get their dollar bonds sold. Mongolyn Alt, one of the 2 issuers planning to sell such debt, has pulled its 5-year offering in a sign of waning demand for junk-rated credit. The coal miner's bonds, provisionally rated in highly speculative territory by \uc2 Fitch\uc2 , couldn't attract enough investors even at 12%. Meanwhile, Mongolian Mortgage--rated the same as the country at B3--has gone silent after meeting investors who weren't satisfied with the 8% yield it was offering, according to a source familiar with the situation. (manju.dalal@wsj.com; @manjudalalsg)\par \par \uc2 0720 GMT - \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President \uc2 Mario Draghi\uc2  is expected to keep a positive tone at his news conference Thursday, despite the recent setback of some economic indicators, say \uc2 KBC Bank\uc2  analysts. The combination of growth and inflation comments might signal rising awareness in favor of policy normalization to build a "war chest" for future downturns, according to analysts, who say they will be looking for clues of that direction. From a market perspective, \uc2 KBC\uc2  sees the probability of a dovish surprise as "rather low," while a "neutral" meeting shouldn't hamper a continued rise in bond yields. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha)\par \par \uc2 0705 GMT - \uc2 Huawei\uc2  has pulled what would have been its 1st euro-denomiated bond deal after reports of a US investigation into possible violation of Iran sanctions by the Chinese telecom-equipment heavyweight. It cancelled the EUR500 million ($609 million) 5-year offering just minutes after announcing final guidance when orders had already topped EUR2 billion. Bankers close to the deal said the cash-rich company hasn't ruled out coming back to the market, but for now it wants to stay on the sidelines until investors can get full clarity of the situation. \uc2 Huawei\uc2  's $500 million 2027s were recently bid at 92.92 cents on a dollar, yielding around 5%; they were at 93.36 cents last night. The company had already dropped its dollar-bond tranche yesterday. (manju.dalal@wsj.com; @manjudalalsg)\par \par \uc2 0701 GMT - EUR/USD rises 0.2% to 1.2187 and may increase further after the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meeting, \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  says. The \uc2 ECB\uc2  is likely to keep its constructive baseline scenario, potentially acknowledging increased uncertainty around its central estimates, but maintaining the balance of risks broadly unchanged, according to the bank. "This outcome may well fall short of the dovish expectations that the market has been gearing up for, and consequently, EUR/USD could come under significant upside pressure," \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  says. One-week and three-month risk reversals in EUR/USD pointed to a downward move in EUR/USD and so there is room for a reversal. (olga.cotaga@wsj.com; @OlgaCotaga)\par \par \uc2 0650 GMT - Oil at 3 1/2-year highs has more analysts anticipating an earlier-than-expected rate hike by \uc2 India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  . Hawkish undertones in the April meeting minutes, sticky core inflation and a sliding rupee have raised the scope for a pre-emptive rate hike in 3Q, contends DBS. Yesterday, \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  said one could come as early as June. Higher oil prices risks widening India's trade and current-account deficits, notes DBS. (debiprasad.nayak@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 26, 2018 05:04 ET (09:04 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020180426ee4q0014y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
