\par\par \b \uc2 Indonesia Stocks at 6-Mo Low; Bank Rout Continues -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 163 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 April 2018\par 11:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2018, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0904 GMT - A day after logging their worst day in 17 months, Indonesia stocks fared even worse today as banks in the country continued to see their share prices shrivel amid further selling of the rupiah. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been hitting its lowest levels since early 2016's global stock swoon despite \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , raising the prospect of \uc2 Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  boosting rates to curb capital outflows. The JSX ended down 2.8% at 5909.20, a 6-month closing low. Rakyat, the country's biggest bank by assets, fell another 5.1% today, putting the week's swoon at 14%, while No. 2 Mandiri slid 5.2% to put its decline during the past 4 days at 15%. With modest further declines tomorrow, both could log their worst weeks since 2008. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com; @kevinkingsbury)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 April 26, 2018 05:04 ET (09:04 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020180426ee4q0019d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
