\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2- \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia \highlight\uc2  c.bank  \highlight22\uc2 intervenes \highlight\uc2  with 'sizeable amount' to defend rupiah\b0\par\par\uc2 619 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 April 2018\par 12:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2018 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * Rupiah touches 2-year low on Tuesday, bond yield jumps\par \par \uc2 * C.bank to continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , governor says\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves down $6 bln at end-March from end-Jan, partly due to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  (Adds Bank Mandiri's CEO comment)\par \par \uc2 By Gayatri Suroyo and Fransiska Nangoy\par \par \uc2 JAKARTA, April 24 (Reuters) - \uc2 Indonesia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been buying sovereign bonds and selling foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in "quite a sizeable amount" to halt the rupiah's depreciation since last week and will continue to stabilise the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , its governor said on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Governor \uc2 Agus Martowardojo\uc2  said in a statement \uc2 Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's (BI) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had made the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  more stable this week, following a 0.7 percent fall against the dollar on Friday.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, the rupiah touched a new two-year low of 13,899 a dollar, though volatility in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has subsided.\par \par \uc2 "BI will continue to be present in the market to maintain the rupiah's stability to reflect its fundamentals," the governor said, adding that the rupiah's swings so far this year have been smaller than those of the Brazilian real, Indian rupee and Philippine peso.\par \par \uc2 BI's holdings of sovereign bonds increased by 7.12 trillion rupiah ($512.78 million) in two trading days to Monday, while foreigners sold a net 3.18 trillion rupiah in the sovereign bond market, according to \uc2 Indonesia's finance ministry\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The yield on the benchmark 10-year sovereign bond jumped to 6.887 percent on Tuesday from 6.683 percent at the opening of Friday's session.\par \par \uc2 Foreigners sold a combined 1.9 trillion rupiah of Indonesian company stocks from Friday to Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 In a sign of cautious investor sentiment, the finance ministry said it had raised 6.15 trillion rupiah in a bond auction on Tuesday, less than half its indicative target of 17 trillion rupiah and the smallest amount of its biweekly auctions so far this year.\par \par \uc2 Indonesia's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves were $126 billion at the end of March, $6 billion lower than the January level. BI has previously said it used the reserves partly for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but has never revealed details.\par \par \uc2 The March reserves level was equal to 7.9 months of imports, far above the international adequacy standard of three months, BI said.\par \par \uc2 BI is under pressure to keep the rupiah stable with some economists and market players pointing to the need to raise its benchmark interest rate.\par \par \uc2 Kartika Wirjoatmodjo, chief executive of Indonesia's second-biggest bank by assets \uc2 Bank Mandiri\uc2 , said BI's policy rate will have to follow that of the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  in the next one to three months to respond to the weakening rupiah.\par \par \uc2 "If BI's policy direction shows an alignment with the Fed's rates in the second quarter, the dollar's exchange rate could be in a range of 13,700-13,900 rupiah by the end of the year," he told reporters.\par \par \uc2 BI kept its key interest rate on hold in a policy meeting last week, but put out a statement that several economists thought was more hawkish.\par \par \uc2 In his Tuesday statement, Governor Martowardojo said a number of global developments could continue to trigger capital outflows from Indonesia's bond and stock markets, including rising interest rates in the U.S., a possible trade war between U.S. and China and higher oil prices.\par \par \uc2 Domestically, rising foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  demand from companies to pay for imports and foreign debt could also pressure the rupiah, he said. ($1 = 13,885.0000 rupiah) (Additional reporting by Nilufar Rizki and Cindy Silviana; Editing by Sam Holmes and Jacqueline Wong) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2018-4-24T11:59:54.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020180424ee4o00aj3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
