\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Indonesia c.bank holds rates, keeps eye on rupiah\b0\par\par\uc2 572 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2018\par 12:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2018 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * Key rate kept at 4.25 pct, its level since September\par \par \uc2 * C.bank says economy should grow faster in Q1 than year earlier\par \par \uc2 * Pledges to keep rupiah's level in line with fundamentals\par \par \uc2 By Nilufar Rizki and Maikel Jefriando\par \par \uc2 JAKARTA, March 22 (Reuters) - \uc2 Indonesia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday kept its policy rate unchanged, as expected, saying its stance was sufficient to support the economy and pledged to keep the rupiah \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in line with fundamentals.\par \par \uc2 The decision came shortly after the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 , as anticipated, hiked U.S. rates. Central banks in New Zealand, the Philippines and Taiwan also held their policy rates on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bank Indonesia (BI) \uc2 decision "is consistent with efforts to maintain stability... and pushes domestic economic recovery," spokesman Agusman told reporters.\par \par \uc2 BI held its seven-day reverse repurchase rate at 4.25 percent, where it has been since a 25 basis point cut in September.\par \par \uc2 "We also view the current policy stance to be appropriate \u8211\'20\'13 the mediocre growth momentum is not related to the level of interest rates but other structural issues," ANZ economist \uc2 Sanjay Mathur\uc2  said in a note.\par \par \uc2 BI cut the benchmark by 200 basis points in 2016 and 2017. The last time it hiked the key rate was November 2014.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it expected first quarter economic growth to be better than one year earlier. BI is targeting GDP growth of 5.1-5.5 percent this year, still well below President \uc2 Joko Widodo\uc2 's target of 7 percent annual growth.\par \par \uc2 MORE RISKS, UNCERTAINTY\par \par \uc2 BI said it remains aware of the "increasing risk of global financial market uncertainty" and will keep taking steps to "stabilize the exchange rate in accordance with its fundamental value while maintaining market mechanisms."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials have said BI is committed to maintaining a presence in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to stabilise the rupiah, but said room for further easing of monetary policy is limited.\par \par \uc2 The deposit facility and lending facility rate were also unchanged, at 3.50 percent and 5.00 percent, respectively.\par \par \uc2 Some economists say BI will eventually have to follow the Fed and hike rates to support the rupiah, which this month fell to 13,800 to the dollar, its weakest in more than two years.\par \par \uc2 But so far, inflation is in check, helping BI hold the key rate as it tries to help boost sluggish lending and growth. Annual credit expansion was single-digit in 2017, while tepid consumption kept growth from rising to much above 5 percent.\par \par \uc2 Indonesia's loans at the end of January were 7.4 percent larger than a year earlier, the financial regulator said on Thursday, down from 8.35 percent in December 2017.\par \par \uc2 The rupiah, which has weakened about 1.2 percent this year against the dollar, on Thursday afternoon was trading at about 13,750 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 Yoga Affandi, a BI director, said it does not target a rupiah level, "but it's in line with the inflation targeting framework".\par \par \uc2 February's annual headline inflation rate was 3.18 percent, well within BI's target range.\par \par \uc2 BI officials said the $3.9 billion drop in \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves in February stemmed mainly from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent the rupiah from weakening too sharply. (Additional reporting by Tabita Diela Writing by Ed Davies; Editing by Richard Borsuk) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2018-3-22T12:35:08.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020180322ee3m00f4w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
